---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Half one of the `count()` guard (#973). This rule decides, and records, whether
# a `count()` in this method body is one the JVM can ELIDE; `142`'s half then
# keeps the pipeline native when it is.
#
# `ReferencePipeline.count()` is allowed to skip the pipeline entirely when the
# source is SIZED and no stage changes the element count, and it does — it just
# asks the spliterator for its size. `map`, `peek` and `boxed` all preserve
# SIZED, so under a bare `count()` their lambdas never run:
#
#   Stream.of("a", "bb").map(s -> { log(s); return s.length(); }).count()
#
# never calls `log`. `mapMulti` clears SIZED by design, so once the chain is
# fused into one the same terminal has to traverse and `log` runs for every
# element — an exception thrown in such a lambda goes the same way, invisible
# before the rewrite and thrown after it. Both are within the specification,
# since `count()` is documented as possibly not executing the pipeline, but it
# is the one case where fusing ADDS work instead of removing it, and in exactly
# that case the fusion buys nothing at all: the pipeline it optimises was never
# going to run.
#
# So the guard is free where it fires, and that is the whole argument for
# taking the conservative branch the issue offers rather than only documenting
# the behaviour.
#
# WHY THIS RUNS FIRST, ahead of even the label cleanups: `142` needs to know
# that NO count-changing stage sits in front of the terminal, and a phino
# pattern cannot test a binding group for an ABSENCE. The dual, positive route
# every other guard here takes (`222`, `224`, `454`) is to match the thing that
# is present — so this rule matches a RESIZING call and stamps the `count()`
# with `traverses ↦ Φ.true`, and `142` is closed on the unstamped opcode and
# therefore fires only when no such call was found. That works only while every
# operation is still a raw opcode carrying its own name: by `111` a `filter`,
# `takeWhile`, `flatMap` or `mapMulti` has been swallowed into a Φ.hone.lambda
# and its `invokeinterface` is gone, and `103`-`108` swallow a method-reference
# one even earlier. Sorting before `101-remove-self-reference-labels` puts this
# rule ahead of all of them, where the body is still exactly what jeo read out
# of the class file.
#
# The name list is every operation that CLEARS SIZED, which is not the same as
# every operation that changes the element count, and the difference is the
# whole subtlety here. `limit` and `skip` change the count and yet keep the
# elision, because the JDK's SliceOps can compute the new size from the old
# one; `sorted` reorders and keeps it too. Measured against the JDK rather than
# reasoned about — `s.limit(3).map(f).count()` runs `f` zero times, while
# `s.filter(p).map(f).count()` runs it once per element — the dividing line is:
#
#   keeps the elision   none limit skip sorted peek unordered parallel
#   clears it           filter distinct takeWhile dropWhile flatMap mapMulti
#
# so `limit` and `skip` belong on neither this list nor nowhere: they are
# handled by `142` leaving the stages around them native. Getting the list wrong
# is safe in one direction only, and that is the direction it errs: a
# SIZED-clearing operation MISSING from it means no stamp, so `142` keeps the
# chain native and a fusion that would have been free is forfeited — an
# optimisation lost, never a behaviour changed. A SIZED-preserving one wrongly
# ADDED would suppress the guard and let the regression back in, so nothing
# joins this list that has not been measured to clear SIZED.
#
# Detection is method-local and over-broad, exactly as `224`'s and `454`'s are,
# and here too that errs safe: a `filter` on an unrelated, locally-consumed
# stream in the same method stamps this `count()` as well, which only means the
# chain in front of it keeps a fusion it could have kept anyway. The owner of
# the resizing call must be a stream interface, though (#1082): an
# `Optional.filter` or a user type's `distinct()` clears nothing, and a stamp
# taken from it lets the chain fuse in front of a count the JVM would elide.
#
# The stamp is a fifth operand on the opcode, the same trick `441`/`442`/`454`
# use: jeo ignores it when assembling, so the bytecode is an ordinary
# `Stream.count()` call. The pattern is closed on the four-operand form jeo
# emits, so a stamped opcode stops matching here and this rule cannot re-fire on
# its own output.
name: mark-traversing-count
pattern: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-resize ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-rop,
      𝜏-rclass ↦ 𝑒-rclass,
      𝜏-rmethod ↦ 𝑒-rmethod,
      𝐵-resize-rest
    ⟧,
    𝐵-between,
    𝜏-count ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokeinterface,
      𝜏-cclass ↦ 𝑒-cclass,
      𝜏-cmethod ↦ "count",
      𝜏-csignature ↦ "()J",
      𝜏-ciface ↦ 𝑒-ciface
    ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-resize ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.𝜏-rop,
      𝜏-rclass ↦ 𝑒-rclass,
      𝜏-rmethod ↦ 𝑒-rmethod,
      𝐵-resize-rest
    ⟧,
    𝐵-between,
    𝜏-count ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokeinterface,
      𝜏-cclass ↦ 𝑒-cclass,
      𝜏-cmethod ↦ "count",
      𝜏-csignature ↦ "()J",
      𝜏-ciface ↦ 𝑒-ciface,
      traverses ↦ Φ.true
    ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
when:
  and:
    - or:
        - eq: [𝜏-rop, 'invokeinterface']
        - eq: [𝜏-rop, 'invokevirtual']
    - matches:
        - '^(filter|distinct|takeWhile|dropWhile|flatMap|flatMapTo(Int|Long|Double)|mapMulti|mapMultiTo(Int|Long|Double|Obj))$'
        - 𝑒-rmethod
    - matches:
        - '^java/util/stream/(Stream|IntStream|LongStream|DoubleStream)$'
        - 𝑒-rclass
